<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Netherlands Trading Society</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1824–1964</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nederlandsche Handel-Maatschappij</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> Trading Society (Nederlandsche Handel-Maatschappij) was a trading and financial company founded in 1824 on the initiative of King William I to revive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> commerce. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultivation-system">Cultivation System</a> it held the monopoly on shipping and selling in the Netherlands the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> that the government collected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. It later became a major colonial bank and merged into ABN in 1964, a forerunner of ABN AMRO.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/netherlands-trading-society.jpg" alt="Netherlands Trading Society" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jane023 (talk) 09:51, 20 February 2011 (UTC) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Bazel_Amsterdam_NHM_Nederlandsche_Handel-Maatschappij.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
